1
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:11,380
And the old woman climbed to
the upper room, laughing aloud,

2
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:14,680
to tell her mistress that her own
dear husband was in the house;

3
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:18,300
her knees were pumping fast, her feet stumbled with hurry.

4
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:21,820
She stood over Penelope's head and spoke to her:

5
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:27,500
"Wake, Penelope, dear child — so you can see with
your own eyes what you have longed for all your days!

6
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:31,100
Odysseus is here — he has come home — however late.

7
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:33,720
And he has killed the lordly suitors, the men who plagued

8
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:37,400
his house, and ate his goods, and bullied his son."

9
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:39,900
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered her:

10
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:46,000
"Dear nurse, the gods have made you mad. The gods
can strike even the full of sense to foolishness,

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,400
and set the empty-headed on the road of wisdom:

12
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:51,860
they have unstrung you. Your mind was straight before.

13
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:58,000
Why do you mock me, when my heart is all one grief,
telling wild tales — and shake me out of the sleep,

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,820
the sweet sleep that had bound me,
folding my eyelids under?

15
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:07,540
I have not slept so sound since the day Odysseus
went to look on Evil-Ilium, never to be named.

16
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:11,000
Go on — down with you now, back to the hall.

17
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,560
If any other of my women had come to me with
news like this, and shaken me out of sleep,

18
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:17,640
I would soon have sent her packing back to the hall

19
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:21,860
and roughly. You — your old
age spares you that, at least."

20
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:24,180
Then the dear nurse Eurycleia answered her:

21
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:27,160
"I do not mock you, dear child. It is true:

22
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:30,520
Odysseus is here — he has come home, just as I tell you —

23
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:33,700
the stranger, the one they all dishonored in the hall.

24
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:38,900
Telemachus knew long since that he was here,
but kept his father's purposes hidden, cool of head,

25
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:42,340
till he should punish the violence
of those overbearing men."

26
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:45,900
So she spoke; and Penelope, all joy, sprang out of bed

27
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:49,400
and clung to the old woman,
tears spilling from her eyelids,

28
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:52,220
and lifting her voice she spoke winged words to her:

29
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:54,340
"Come then, dear nurse — tell me truly:

30
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:59,480
if he has really come home, as you say he has,
how did he lay his hands on the shameless suitors —

31
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:02,740
one man alone, when they were always in there in a pack?"

32
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:04,940
Then the dear nurse Eurycleia answered her:

33
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:08,260
"I did not see, I did not ask. I heard only the groaning

34
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:11,480
of men being killed. We sat in
the corner of our strong-built

35
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:15,360
quarters, struck dumb —
the close-fitted doors held us in —

36
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:20,840
until the hour your son called me out of the hall,
Telemachus: his father had sent him to call me.

37
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:26,220
And then I found Odysseus standing among the bodies of
the slain; and they, around him, on the trodden floor

38
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:29,480
lay heaped on one another.
It would have warmed your heart —

39
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:34,480
Now they are all together at the courtyard gates,
and he is purging the beautiful house with sulfur,

40
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:37,720
and has built a great fire; and he sent me to call you.

41
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:40,560
Come with me now — so both of you can set your dear hearts

42
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:44,540
in the way of gladness at last:
you have suffered so much evil.

43
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:47,780
Now, this time, the long desire is accomplished:

44
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:52,280
he has come himself, alive,
to his own hearth; and he has found you

45
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:56,380
and his son still in the halls;
and the men who wronged him,

46
00:02:56,380 --> 00:02:59,680
the suitors — he has punished them all in his own house."

47
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:02,240
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered her:

48
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:05,900
"Dear nurse — no crowing yet. Hold back your laughter.

49
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:12,780
You know how welcome the sight of him would be to
everyone in this house — most of all to me and to our son.

50
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:15,860
But this tale cannot be true, not as you tell it:

51
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:19,460
no — some one of the immortals
has killed the lordly suitors,

52
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:23,220
outraged at their heart-stinging
insolence and their evil deeds.

53
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:28,320
For they honored nobody on this earth,
bad man or good, whoever came among them;

54
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:32,300
so through their own blind folly
they have suffered. But Odysseus —

55
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:37,320
far away he has lost his homecoming,
lost out of Achaea; he himself is lost."

56
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:40,240
Then the dear nurse Eurycleia answered her:

57
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:43,660
"My child, what a word has
slipped the fence of your teeth!

58
00:03:43,660 --> 00:03:47,420
Your husband is here —
at his own hearthside — and you declare

59
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:52,420
he will never come home!
Your heart was always unbelieving.

60
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:56,580
Come then — I will give you another sign, and a plain one:

61
00:03:56,580 --> 00:04:01,020
the scar the boar dealt him long ago with its white tusk.

62
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:04,220
I found it as I washed him, and I wanted to tell you —

63
00:04:04,220 --> 00:04:07,280
you yourself — but he clapped his hand across my mouth

64
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:11,560
and would not let me speak: his mind is full of cunning.

65
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:14,140
But come. And I will stake my own life on it:

66
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:18,500
if I am deceiving you, kill me by
the most pitiful death there is."

67
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:21,240
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered her:

68
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:24,620
"Dear nurse — the counsels of the gods who are forever:

69
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:28,440
it is hard for you to fathom them,
sharp-witted as you are.

70
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:35,180
But even so, let us go down to my son, so I may see
the suitors dead — and the man who killed them."

71
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:38,240
So speaking she went down from
the upper room; and her heart

72
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:41,700
churned: should she question her
dear husband from a distance,

73
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:45,340
or go to him, and take his head and hands, and kiss them?

74
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:48,680
And when she came in, and crossed the stone threshold,

75
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:52,500
she sat down opposite Odysseus, in the light of the fire,

76
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:58,000
against the other wall. And he, by the tall pillar,
sat looking at the ground, waiting to learn whether

77
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,860
his strong-built wife would speak
to him, once her eyes had seen.

78
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:06,800
But she sat a long while silent,
wonder flooding her heart:

79
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:10,260
with her eyes now she would gaze at him, full in the face;

80
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:14,380
now fail to know him — in the bad clothes on his body.

81
00:05:14,380 --> 00:05:17,920
And Telemachus rebuked her,
and spoke a word, and named her:

82
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:24,460
"Mother mine — no mother! — with your unbending heart,
why do you hold apart from my father so? Why not

83
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:28,640
sit close beside him, and question him,
and ask him everything?

84
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:34,700
No other woman would harden her heart like this,
and stand apart from a husband who, after suffering much,

85
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:37,480
came in the twentieth year to his own fatherland.

86
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:41,520
But your heart is harder than stone —
as it has always been."

87
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:44,240
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

88
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:48,040
"My child, the heart in my breast is stunned to stillness:

89
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:54,220
I cannot speak one word to him, cannot ask a question,
cannot look him in the face. But if he is truly

90
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:57,620
Odysseus — if he has come home — then surely we two

91
00:05:57,620 --> 00:06:00,940
shall know one another,
and better than anyone: for we have

92
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:06,280
signs between us — hidden signs,
that we two know and no one else."

93
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:12,940
So she spoke; and much-enduring noble Odysseus smiled,
and quickly he spoke winged words to Telemachus:

94
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:18,120
"Telemachus — let your mother test me in the
halls as she will: she will soon see more clearly.

95
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:21,340
Now, because I am filthy,
and the clothes on my body are bad,

96
00:06:21,340 --> 00:06:24,920
she holds me cheap, and will not yet say I am the man.

97
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:28,060
But we — let us think how this can come out best.

98
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:33,900
For a man who has killed even one man in the land —
one man, with few behind him to avenge him after —

99
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:37,260
goes into exile, leaving his kin and his fathers' country.

100
00:06:37,260 --> 00:06:41,680
And we have killed the prop of the city —
far the noblest of the young men in Ithaca.

101
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:44,260
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

102
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:50,500
"You look to it yourself, dear father. Yours,
they say, is the best mind among mankind: no mortal man

103
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:52,640
could set himself against you there.

104
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:56,640
As for us — we are with you,
blazing for it; and I say we shall not

105
00:06:56,640 --> 00:07:00,320
come short in courage, such strength as is in us."

106
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:02,940
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

107
00:07:02,940 --> 00:07:05,540
"Then I will say how it seems best to me.

108
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:12,380
First: bathe yourselves, and put clean tunics on,
and tell the serving-women in the halls to dress;

109
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:16,540
and let the godlike singer,
with the clear lyre in his hands,

110
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:19,100
lead us all in the steps of the playful dance —

111
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:22,580
so that anyone who hears it from
outside will say, 'A wedding!' —

112
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:25,840
a passer-by on the road,
or one of our neighbors round about —

113
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:30,820
so that the wide rumor of the suitors' killing does
not run down through the town before we can get away

114
00:07:30,820 --> 00:07:34,520
out to our orchard-farm among the trees. There, after,

115
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:37,420
we shall see what profit the
Olympian puts into our hands."

116
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:41,320
So he spoke; and they listened well and obeyed.

117
00:07:41,320 --> 00:07:47,540
First they bathed themselves, and put clean tunics on,
and the women dressed; and the godlike singer took up

118
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:53,400
the hollow lyre, and woke in all of them the craving
for the sweetness of song and the blameless dance.

119
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:58,780
And the great house rang around them to the footfalls
of men at play and fair-belted women dancing;

120
00:07:58,780 --> 00:08:01,640
and a man would say, hearing it from outside the house:

121
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:05,040
"So! Someone has married the much-wooed queen at last.

122
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:07,240
Hard woman — she had not the heart to keep her wedded

123
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:10,580
husband's great house through to the end, until he came."

124
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:14,460
So a man would say. And they
did not know how things stood.

125
00:08:14,460 --> 00:08:17,160
And meanwhile, inside his house, great-hearted Odysseus

126
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:20,700
was bathed by the housekeeper
Eurynome, and rubbed with oil,

127
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:23,980
and she put a beautiful mantle round him, and a tunic;

128
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:27,780
and over him, from the head down,
Athena poured great beauty:

129
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:30,520
taller to look at, and broader, and down from his head

130
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:34,340
she made the locks flow in curls,
like the hyacinth flower.

131
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:39,240
As when a man lays gold over silver — a craftsman
whom Hephaestus and Pallas Athena have taught

132
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:45,800
art of every kind, and he finishes works full of grace —
so she poured grace over his head and shoulders.

133
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:49,000
Out of the bath he came, in build like the immortals,

134
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:54,800
and sat back down on the chair from which he had risen,
opposite his wife, and spoke his word to her:

135
00:08:54,800 --> 00:09:00,120
"Strange woman! To you beyond all womankind the
dwellers on Olympus gave an unyielding heart.

136
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:05,320
No other woman would harden her heart like this,
and stand apart from a husband who, after suffering much,

137
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:07,840
came in the twentieth year to his own fatherland.

138
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:11,040
Well then, nurse — make me up a bed, so I can lie down

139
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:14,740
by myself at least. The heart
in this one's breast is iron."

140
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:17,360
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

141
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:21,120
"Strange man! I am not being proud,
and I am not slighting you —

142
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:27,300
nor am I too much amazed. I know very well what you
were when you went from Ithaca on your long-oared ship.

143
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:30,880
Well then — Eurycleia: make up the packed bedstead for him

144
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:33,620
outside the well-built bridal chamber — the bed he made

145
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:37,320
with his own hands. Move the
packed bedstead out there for him,

146
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:41,780
and pile the bedding on: the fleeces,
the cloaks, the shining blankets."

147
00:09:41,780 --> 00:09:47,380
So she spoke — testing her husband. And Odysseus
flared out in anger at his true-hearted wife:

148
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:50,180
"Woman — that is a wounding word you have spoken!

149
00:09:50,180 --> 00:09:52,900
Who has set my bed somewhere else? That would be hard

150
00:09:52,900 --> 00:09:56,040
even for a skilled workman — unless a god in person came

151
00:09:56,040 --> 00:09:59,320
and set it down, at his easy will, in some other place:

152
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:02,400
no living mortal man, not one in his best young strength,

153
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:05,800
could lightly lever it aside. For a great sign is built

154
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:09,800
into that worked bed — and I made it: I, no other man.

155
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:13,560
A bush of olive grew inside the
courtyard fence, long-leaved,

156
00:10:13,560 --> 00:10:17,020
full-grown and flourishing, its trunk thick as a pillar.

157
00:10:17,020 --> 00:10:20,620
Around it I built our chamber,
and I worked at it till it was done —

158
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:26,100
close-set stone; and I roofed it well above,
and hung the jointed doors on it, close-fitting.

159
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:29,860
Then — and only then — I cut back
the long-leaved olive's crown,

160
00:10:29,860 --> 00:10:32,840
trimmed the trunk up from the root,
and with the bronze I planed it

161
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:36,380
smooth all round, with skill, and trued it to the line:

162
00:10:36,380 --> 00:10:40,680
a bedpost worked from the living tree.
And I bored it all with the auger,

163
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:44,860
and starting from that post I planed
out the bed, until I finished,

164
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:47,240
inlaying it with gold and silver and ivory,

165
00:10:47,240 --> 00:10:53,100
and I stretched across its frame an
oxhide strap, bright with crimson.

166
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:56,220
There is our sign — I show it to you. What I do not know,

167
00:10:56,220 --> 00:11:00,340
woman, is whether my bed still stands fast, or by now

168
00:11:00,340 --> 00:11:04,440
some man has cut the olive off at the root and moved it."

169
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:09,080
So he spoke; and her knees went slack
where she sat, and her dear heart,

170
00:11:09,080 --> 00:11:13,060
as she recognized the tokens Odysseus described so surely;

171
00:11:13,060 --> 00:11:16,260
and bursting into tears she ran straight to him, and threw

172
00:11:16,260 --> 00:11:20,260
her arms around Odysseus' neck,
and kissed his head, and said:

173
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:23,460
"Do not be angry with me, Odysseus — you who in everything

174
00:11:23,460 --> 00:11:27,360
understood the most of men.
It was the gods who gave us sorrow —

175
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:30,600
the gods, who begrudged that we two, staying side by side,

176
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:34,440
should have the joy of our youth
and come to the doorsill of old age.

177
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:37,780
But do not be angry with me now, do not hold it against me

178
00:11:37,780 --> 00:11:41,100
that at first, when I saw you,
I did not welcome you like this.

179
00:11:41,100 --> 00:11:44,200
For always the heart here in my breast kept shivering:

180
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:47,540
terror that some man would come
and cheat me with his talk —

181
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:50,640
and men enough there are who plot such wicked profits.

182
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:56,180
Not even Argive Helen, daughter of Zeus, would have
joined in love and bed with a man from another land

183
00:11:56,180 --> 00:12:01,860
had she known that the warlike sons of the Achaeans
would carry her home again to her own dear country.

184
00:12:01,860 --> 00:12:04,900
A god it was who stirred her
to the shameful thing she did;

185
00:12:04,900 --> 00:12:08,060
and not until too late that she laid to heart that folly,

186
00:12:08,060 --> 00:12:11,980
the ruinous folly from which
grief first came — to us as well.

187
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:17,900
But now — now you have told over the plain tokens of
our own bed, which no other mortal has laid eyes on:

188
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:24,360
only you and I, and one single serving-woman, Actoris,
whom my father gave me when I first came here,

189
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:30,480
who kept the door of our close-built bridal chamber —
now you persuade my heart, unbending as it is."

190
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:34,440
So she spoke; and in him she stirred
a still greater longing to weep,

191
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:38,800
and he wept, holding the wife of
his heart, the true, the steadfast.

192
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:44,300
And as when land shows welcome to men swimming for it —
men whose well-made ship Poseidon has smashed

193
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:47,460
on the open sea, driving it under wind and heavy wave,

194
00:12:47,460 --> 00:12:53,000
and few of them swim clear of the gray sea to the shore,
their bodies caked with all the brine of it,

195
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,660
and glad at last they step on land, escaped from evil:

196
00:12:56,660 --> 00:12:59,780
so welcome was her husband to her,
as her eyes gazed at him,

197
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:03,060
and she could not once let her
white arms go from his neck.

198
00:13:03,060 --> 00:13:06,260
And Dawn of the fingers of rose
would have risen on their weeping,

199
00:13:06,260 --> 00:13:10,020
had not the goddess thought of
one thing more — gray-eyed Athena:

200
00:13:10,020 --> 00:13:13,000
she held the long night back
at the world's edge, and penned

201
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:18,760
gold-throned Dawn at Ocean, and would not let her yoke
her quick-footed horses that carry the light to men,

202
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:21,720
Lampus and Phaethon, the colts that draw the Dawn.

203
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:25,060
And then Odysseus of the many designs spoke to his wife:

204
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:27,960
"Lady — we have not yet come to the end of every trial:

205
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:34,020
there is still to be one labor past all measure,
long and hard, and I must carry it through to the last.

206
00:13:34,020 --> 00:13:39,060
For so the ghost of Teiresias prophesied to
me the day I went down into the house of Hades

207
00:13:39,060 --> 00:13:41,900
seeking a homecoming for my companions and myself.

208
00:13:41,900 --> 00:13:47,820
But come, wife — let us go to bed, so that at last
we can lie down and take the sweetness of our sleep."

209
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:50,640
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

210
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:55,180
"Your bed is there for you whenever your heart
wishes it — now that the gods have brought you home

211
00:13:55,180 --> 00:13:57,740
to your own well-built house and your fathers' land.

212
00:13:57,740 --> 00:14:01,720
But since you have thought of it —
since a god has put it in your heart —

213
00:14:01,720 --> 00:14:07,680
come, tell me the trial: for I shall hear of it later
anyway, I think, and it is no worse to know it now."

214
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:10,440
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

215
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:16,160
"Strange woman — why do you press me so, and order
me to speak? Well, I will tell it, and hide nothing.

216
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:19,040
Your heart will take no joy in it; no more does mine:

217
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:24,360
for he ordered me on through many towns of mortals yet,
traveling with a well-shaped oar in my hands,

218
00:14:24,360 --> 00:14:29,600
until I come to men who know nothing of the sea,
men who eat no food that is mixed with salt,

219
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:34,800
who know nothing of red-cheeked ships, nothing
of well-shaped oars, which are wings for ships.

220
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:40,460
And he told me a sign, unmistakable.
I will not hide it from you:

221
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:46,320
when another traveler, meeting me on the road,
says I carry a winnowing-fan on my shining shoulder,

222
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:52,600
then and there he ordered me to fix the oar in the
earth and offer fine victims to the lord Poseidon —

223
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:58,780
a ram, a bull, and a boar that has mounted sows —
and turn away home, and offer holy hecatombs

224
00:14:58,780 --> 00:15:05,360
to the deathless gods who hold wide heaven,
to all of them, each in order. And death will come to me

225
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:11,780
from the sea, so gentle a death, and take me worn
down under sleek old age; and around me the peoples

226
00:15:11,780 --> 00:15:15,750
will be prosperous. All this,
he said, will be accomplished."

227
00:15:15,750 --> 00:15:18,590
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

228
00:15:18,590 --> 00:15:21,510
"If the gods are really bringing on a better old age,

229
00:15:21,510 --> 00:15:24,210
then there is hope there will
be an escape from our evils."

230
00:15:24,210 --> 00:15:26,790
So they spoke to one another of such things.

231
00:15:26,790 --> 00:15:29,350
And meanwhile Eurynome and the nurse made up the bed

232
00:15:29,350 --> 00:15:33,010
with soft coverings, under the light of blazing torches.

233
00:15:33,010 --> 00:15:36,270
And when they had spread the
packed bedstead, working quickly,

234
00:15:36,270 --> 00:15:41,170
the old woman went away to her own room to sleep,
and Eurynome the chamber-woman led them on

235
00:15:41,170 --> 00:15:46,450
their way to bed, a torch in her hands;
she brought them to the chamber, and went back. And they —

236
00:15:46,450 --> 00:15:49,710
glad, they came to the rite of their own old bed.

237
00:15:49,710 --> 00:15:55,330
And Telemachus and the cowherd and the swineherd
rested their feet from the dance, and rested the women,

238
00:15:55,330 --> 00:15:58,350
and lay down to sleep themselves
through the shadowy halls.

239
00:15:58,350 --> 00:16:01,770
And when the two had taken their
fill of the loveliness of love,

240
00:16:01,770 --> 00:16:05,310
they took their pleasure in words,
telling each other everything:

241
00:16:05,310 --> 00:16:11,770
she, the shining among women, all she had endured in
the halls, watching that deadly crowd of suitor-men

242
00:16:11,770 --> 00:16:15,070
who on her account kept slaughtering so many beasts,

243
00:16:15,070 --> 00:16:19,530
oxen and fat sheep, while the wine
drained out of the jars by the vatful;

244
00:16:19,530 --> 00:16:22,570
and Zeus-born Odysseus, all the sorrows he had dealt

245
00:16:22,570 --> 00:16:26,170
to other men, and all he had
toiled through in his own grief —

246
00:16:26,170 --> 00:16:29,150
he told it all; and she delighted in the listening,

247
00:16:29,150 --> 00:16:33,110
and no sleep fell upon her eyelids
till he had told everything.

248
00:16:33,110 --> 00:16:39,010
He began with how he beat the Cicones first, and came
from there to the rich plowland of the Lotus-eaters;

249
00:16:39,010 --> 00:16:42,530
and all the Cyclops did,
and how he took the price from him

250
00:16:42,530 --> 00:16:48,090
for the brave companions he had eaten without pity;
and how he came to Aeolus, who received him kindly

251
00:16:48,090 --> 00:16:51,250
and sent him on — but it was not yet his portion to reach

252
00:16:51,250 --> 00:16:55,250
his own dear country: the storm-wind snatched him up again

253
00:16:55,250 --> 00:16:58,490
and drove him, groaning heavily, over the fish-filled sea;

254
00:16:58,490 --> 00:17:01,890
and how he came to Telepylus of the Laestrygonians,

255
00:17:01,890 --> 00:17:04,830
who destroyed his ships and all
his strong-greaved companions —

256
00:17:04,830 --> 00:17:12,790
all of them: Odysseus alone got clear in his black ship;
and he told her Circe's guile and her many wiles,

257
00:17:12,790 --> 00:17:17,810
and how he went down into the dank house of
Hades to consult the ghost of Theban Teiresias,

258
00:17:17,810 --> 00:17:21,130
in his many-benched ship, and saw all his companions,

259
00:17:21,130 --> 00:17:24,890
and his mother, who bore him and
reared him when he was little;

260
00:17:24,890 --> 00:17:31,750
and how he heard the voice of the thronging Sirens,
and came to the Clashing Rocks, and dread Charybdis,

261
00:17:31,750 --> 00:17:35,470
and Scylla, whom men have never yet slipped past unhurt;

262
00:17:35,470 --> 00:17:41,670
and how his companions killed the cattle of the Sun;
and how Zeus who thunders on high struck the fast ship

263
00:17:41,670 --> 00:17:48,530
with a smoking bolt, and his brave companions perished
all together — and he alone escaped the evil fates;

264
00:17:48,530 --> 00:17:52,950
and how he came to the island
of Ogygia and the nymph Calypso,

265
00:17:52,950 --> 00:17:58,950
who kept him there, aching to have him for her husband,
in her hollow caves, and fed him, and kept promising

266
00:17:58,950 --> 00:18:04,710
to make him deathless and ageless all his days —
yet she never persuaded the heart in his breast;

267
00:18:04,710 --> 00:18:10,670
and how, after much suffering, he came to the Phaeacians,
who honored him from the heart, like a god,

268
00:18:10,670 --> 00:18:16,910
and sent him home with a ship to his own dear country,
with gifts of bronze and gold and clothing in plenty.

269
00:18:16,910 --> 00:18:20,510
That was the last word he spoke, as the sweet sleep

270
00:18:20,510 --> 00:18:25,010
that unstrings the limbs leapt on him,
loosing the cares of his heart.

271
00:18:25,010 --> 00:18:28,590
Then the goddess thought of one
thing more, gray-eyed Athena:

272
00:18:28,590 --> 00:18:34,450
when she judged that Odysseus in his heart had taken
his fill of the bed of his wife, and his fill of sleep,

273
00:18:34,450 --> 00:18:38,210
at once she roused the gold-throned
early-born one out of Ocean,

274
00:18:38,210 --> 00:18:45,370
to carry her light to mankind. And Odysseus rose
from the soft bed, and laid this charge on his wife:

275
00:18:45,370 --> 00:18:50,310
"Lady — by now we have had our fill of trials,
the two of us both: you here, weeping over

276
00:18:50,310 --> 00:18:53,530
my homecoming with all its cares,
while Zeus and the other gods

277
00:18:53,530 --> 00:18:56,980
nailed me in pain far from my own country, straining home.

278
00:18:56,980 --> 00:19:03,010
Now that we both have come to the bed we longed for,
you must care for the property I have inside the halls;

279
00:19:03,010 --> 00:19:08,310
as for the flocks the overweening suitors wasted —
many I will raid back myself, and the Achaeans

280
00:19:08,310 --> 00:19:12,090
will give the rest, until they fill up every fold.

281
00:19:12,090 --> 00:19:15,370
But now I am going out to the orchard-farm and its trees,

282
00:19:15,370 --> 00:19:19,190
to see my noble father, who grieves for me so hard.

283
00:19:19,190 --> 00:19:22,610
And on you, wife, I lay this charge — wise as you are:

284
00:19:22,610 --> 00:19:28,630
with the rising sun the story will be out and running —
the suitors, the men I killed here in the halls.

285
00:19:28,630 --> 00:19:34,830
Go up to the upper room with your attendant women
and sit there. Look at no one. Ask no questions."

286
00:19:34,830 --> 00:19:37,350
He spoke, and drew the beautiful armor over his shoulders,

287
00:19:37,350 --> 00:19:41,190
and roused Telemachus and the cowherd and the swineherd,

288
00:19:41,190 --> 00:19:44,630
and told them all to take up
weapons of war in their hands.

289
00:19:44,630 --> 00:19:48,230
They did not disobey: they harnessed themselves in bronze,

290
00:19:48,230 --> 00:19:53,050
and opened the doors, and went out; and Odysseus led.

291
00:19:53,050 --> 00:19:58,872
By now the light was over the earth; but Athena hid
them in night, and led them quickly out of the town.
